"I, II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fb1a477-6708-584e-8d47-1f28bcca55c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196294 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196294 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196294 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a8958c2-0597-58ee-8feb-2e7d57eb8027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196295 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186196295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2283f4ab-474b-589a-b24f-afa516d783bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198180 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198180 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicates:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x):$ $x$ is prime $\\qquad$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is not a multiple of $10$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)?$\n\n- $x$ is not a multiple of $10$ if $x$ is prime\n\n- If $x$ is not a multiple of $10,$ then $x$ is prime\n\n- $x$ is prime implies $x$ is not a multiple of $10$\n\n- $x$ is not a multiple of $10$ only if $x$ is prime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198180 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d20f2ac9-3190-5c58-a0a5-ddcd8c3f5817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198180 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q{:}$\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n![](images/48/482e156a7d3aef3f75a6b51efec5069fc48696749d7f580d4e40d7801a41f90a.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x).$ $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Option II reads\n![](images/fb/fb19cec758144f4ae3ca6580d5171757369a831f6cfc7d940fa95fcfaf2522b9.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x).$ $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Option III reads\n![](images/1f/1f472c122fe745e97fb31328625eccb71515dfee4b99e4bba548bf367cc8cc78.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x).$ $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Option IV reads\n![](images/52/525a046232143aa333ded66fb2bec55ff0011a8f76ef74373b9c6c99abdc3fd3.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x).$ $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198185 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198185 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ as a sentence given the following:\n\n- $P:$ $A \\cup B = A.$\n\n- $Q:$ $B \\subseteq A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4b354bf-6629-5574-9674-fdc3ca707b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198185 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P\\Leftrightarrow Q{:}$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nTherefore, some correct grammatical constructions of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ are the following:\n\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **if and only if** $B \\subseteq A.$\n\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **iff** $B \\subseteq A.$\n\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **is necessary and sufficient for** $B \\subseteq A.$\n\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **is equivalent to** $B \\subseteq A.$\n\n- **If** $A \\cup B = A,$ **then** $B \\subseteq A,$ **and conversely**." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198187 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198187 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $B$ is sufficient for $A$ |  |\n| For $A,$ it is sufficient that $B$ |  |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ |  |\n| For $A,$ it is necessary that $B$ |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198187 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ba22130-b8bb-55d0-83b0-5a3b89bcf876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198187 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S \\Rightarrow N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S\\Rightarrow N{:}$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"$B$ is sufficient for $A$\" means $B\\Rightarrow A.$\n\n- \"For $A,$ it is sufficient that $B$\" means $B\\Rightarrow A.$\n\n- \"$B$ is necessary for $A$\" means $A\\Rightarrow B.$\n\n- \"For $A,$ it is necessary that $B$\" means $A\\Rightarrow B.$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $B$ is sufficient for $A$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}B\\Rightarrow A}$ |\n| For $A,$ it is sufficient that $B$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}B\\Rightarrow A}$ |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}A\\Rightarrow B}$ |\n| For $A,$ it is necessary that $B$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}A\\Rightarrow B}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198188 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198188 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicates:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x):$ $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$ $\\qquad$ $Q(x):$ $x$ is negative\n\nWhich of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)?$\n\n- Whenever $x$ is negative, then $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$\n\n- $x$ is negative whenever $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$\n\n- $x$ is negative, provided that $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198188 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c776807d-efab-534b-b79c-582e4906bb4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198188 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n![](images/21/21fd3acc1e0a8862b0010d28252278e204bfe6c25abb66aed01f31cc0a565642.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x).$ $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Option II reads\n![](images/44/4436567914aa607eae98ac50a467d6d6be1715d7b1f9cdfa0f907dc0df8b99ba.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x).$ $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Option III reads\n![](images/30/305d426cb7c241d68ab4576848ae519429b58e3ae655cb65c23dc74c09d0722e.svg)\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x).$ $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1039 "\"If-Then\", \"If\", \"Only If\", and \"Implies\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198282 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0809c075-e1be-5d49-b1fe-5433114bcf16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1039 "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198283 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1039 "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198284 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1039 "Biconditionals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198285 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7c614609-dae7-5cde-a26a-52104d47ebf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1045 "Grammatical Constructions for Conditional Statements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198539 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fca1ce31-a26b-5dce-bb5a-6769c343dcd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186194820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-171412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $A⇔B$ as a sentence given the following:\n\n- $A:$ $4$ is even.\n\n- $B:$ $4$ is divisible by $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ceecdafd-e8f8-565d-8402-4e6510683f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198699 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P⇔Q:$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the correct grammatical construction of $A⇔B$ is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $4$ is even **if and only if** $4$ is divisible by $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing entries in the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| Whenever $A,$ then $B$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $B$ whenever $A$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $B,$ provided that $A$ | {{field-3}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05c352e2-4bcb-5824-9fdc-8456a5d43256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198847 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the implication $P⇒Q:$\n\n- Whenever $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n\n- $Q,$ provided that $P$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"Whenever $A,$ then $B$\" means $A⇒B$\n\n- \"$B$ whenever $A$\" means $A⇒B$\n\n- \"$B,$ provided that $A$\" means $A⇒B$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| Whenever $A,$ then $B$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $B$ whenever $A$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $B,$ provided that $A$ | $A⇒B$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is **NOT** a grammatical construction for the biconditional $P⇔Q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f117022-4bba-588f-b9ce-097a7a6ade61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198848 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P⇔Q:$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that is NOT a grammatical construction for $P⇔Q$ is \"$P$ is sufficient for $Q$\" (this is the grammatical construction of $P⇒Q).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing values in the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} | $B⇒A$ |\n| {{field-2}} | $B⇒A$ |\n| {{field-3}} | $A⇒B$ |\n| {{field-4}} | $B⇒A$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d07243ef-de43-536c-b5d7-6f2a6a126ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198858 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"$B$ is sufficient for $A$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\n- \"For $A,$ it is sufficient that $B$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\n- \"$B$ is necessary for $A$\" means $A⇒B.$\n\n- \"For $B,$ it is necessary that $A$\" means $B⇒A.$\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | --- |\n| $B\\text{ is sufficient for }A$ | $B⇒A$ |\n| $\\text{For }A,\\text{ it is sufficient that }B$ | $B⇒A$ |\n| $B\\text{ is necessary for }A$ | $A⇒B$ |\n| $\\text{For }B,\\text{ it is necessary that }A$ | $B⇒A$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186196290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $6\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n\n- $6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$\n- $6\\,|\\,x$ is necessary for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "587a7a92-f47b-5043-840d-bba7363fe179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198859 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199096 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199096 #edb/ref 1061 154105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199096 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc7b467a-0513-53be-be39-87ac3ff581c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199096 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199097 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199097 #edb/ref 1061 154106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199097 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f3e80d5-b0a8-5272-b1aa-9f9129558bb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199097 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199098 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199098 #edb/ref 1061 154109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199098 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7854e8a-298b-5869-a1f8-25d564311e6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199098 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199099 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199099 #edb/ref 1061 154110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199099 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8b79784e-6d7f-54b3-911a-d4be6f515464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199099 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199100 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199100 #edb/ref 1061 154113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09e5d0ff-56a1-531b-8c73-bf32f7aba54b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199101 #edb/ref 1061 154114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2322cf93-67c8-548c-b4ef-289711eb1fe0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199102 #edb/ref 1061 154117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39caa146-84bc-5745-84e8-df160bccbda7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199103 #edb/ref 1061 154118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1829f7f4-e5f0-5eae-a3d2-1fb0fa1141b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199569 #edb/ref 1064 "Up to now, we've seen several grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals. In this lesson, we'll review all those that are commonly used.\n\nThe implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ has the following grammatical constructions:\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n\n- $Q$ if $P$\n\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nFor example, \n\n$\\qquad$ **If** $4 \\mid n,$ **then** $2 \\mid n$\n\nis equivalent to the following:\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **implies** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $4 \\mid n$ **only if** $2 \\mid n$\n\n- $2 \\mid n,$ **if** $4 \\mid n$\n\nUsing these grammatical constructions correctly within a particular mathematical context can be tricky. It's important to go through each and understand why it's equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ So, let's do that. We assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- If $P,$ then $Q{:}$ If $P$ is true, then $Q$ *must* be true.\n\n- $P$ only if $Q{:}$ The *only* way that $P$ is true is if $Q$ is also true.\n\n- $Q$ if $P{:}$ $Q$ is *guaranteed* to be true if $P$ is true.\n\n- $P$ implies $Q.$ This is the same as \"If $P,$ then $Q.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199569 #edb/ref 1065 8289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199569 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ce16042-d1e9-5bdf-a5b6-0c6f71ddce4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199569 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199593 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that, in terms of necessary and sufficient conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\textrm{Sufficient}\\phantom{|}}_{P} \\Rightarrow \\underbrace{\\textrm{Necessary}}_{Q}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following equivalent grammatical constructions:\n\n- $P$ is **sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- For $Q,$ it is **sufficient** that $P$\n\n- $Q$ is **necessary** for $P$\n\n- For $P,$ it is **necessary** that $Q$\n\nLet's go back to the following true implication that we encountered in a previous lesson:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \n\\: \\Rightarrow \\: \n\\underbrace{\\big( \\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}\n$$\n\nWe can use the following grammatical constructions in terms of sufficient conditions:\n\n- $a > 0$ is a *sufficient condition* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- $a > 0$ is *sufficient* for $a^2 > 0$\n\n- For $a^2 > 0,$ it is *sufficient* that $a > 0$\n\nThese all mean the same thing, namely that $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0.$\n\nFor necessary conditions, we have:\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary condition* for $a > 0$\n\n- $a^2 > 0$ is *necessary* for $a > 0$\n\n- For $a > 0,$ it is *necessary* that $a^2 > 0$\n\nThese all mean that if $a > 0$ it follows that $a^2 > 0,$ but $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199593 #edb/ref 1065 8353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199593 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f1f0c849-1dba-5a3c-acab-d874bfd0bd9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199593 #edb/ref 1067 "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199594 #edb/ref 1064 "Implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ also can be written using \"whenever\" and \"provided\" as follows:  \n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q$\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P$\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P$\n\nLet's again go through each one and convince ourselves that they're all equivalent to $P\\Rightarrow Q.$ Again, we assume that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is *true* in each case.\n\n- **Whenever** $P,$ then $Q{:}$ Whenever $P$ is true, $Q$ is guaranteed to be true.\n\n- $Q$ **whenever** $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true whenever $P$ is true.\n\n- $Q,$ **provided** that $P{:}$ $Q$ is guaranteed to be true provided that $P$ is true.\n\nLet's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199594 #edb/ref 1065 8354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199594 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4d7ce271-7f3d-51dc-8313-ac1448341658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199594 #edb/ref 1067 "Whenever and Provided That" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199595 #edb/ref 1064 "The grammatical constructions for biconditionals $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$ are slightly more straightforward than for conditionals.\n\nWe've already seen the following constructions:\n\n- $P$ **if and only if** $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **necessary and sufficient** for $Q$\n\n- $P$ is **equivalent** to $Q$\n\nThe following constructions are also commonly used:\n\n- $P$ **iff** $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ **and conversely**\n\nHere, \"iff\" is simply shorthand for \"if and only if.\"\n\nThe construction \"If $P$ then $Q,$ and conversely\" is interesting. Recall that the converse of $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is $Q\\Rightarrow P.$ If $P\\Rightarrow Q$ and $Q\\Rightarrow P$ are both true, we have that $P$ implies $Q$ and $Q$ implies $P,$ i.e., $P\\Leftrightarrow Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199595 #edb/ref 1065 8355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199595 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "034ee104-6c2c-5faf-b260-a171e852673c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199595 #edb/ref 1067 "Biconditionals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421002 #edb/ref 1080 "B⇒A" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421002 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421002 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0320ab27-8c91-593f-863f-a330a38d6971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421003 #edb/ref 1080 "If $P,$ then $Q$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07315abd-4baf-524a-b2c5-1f7b344c1673" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421004 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "093f27fc-7e6f-5b74-9e0f-56f1dcd8da8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421005 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421005 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421005 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a8ce566-6890-582f-a158-b42ec0895248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421006 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421006 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d40fcad-2b54-5f09-b54d-9a821f10c93a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421007 #edb/ref 1080 "A⇒B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d6844ae-27ea-5374-a438-c24d7e669095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421008 #edb/ref 1080 "II and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421008 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421008 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef0d8fa-6fbc-553b-b26f-6d47fc529632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421009 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ implies $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421009 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421009 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "127d75f0-286e-540c-867f-af08497d27bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421010 #edb/ref 1080 "A⇒B" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421010 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186421010 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "163033c6-267c-510a-879d-d8d2a5dd6ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592